  "answer": "To reduce the risk of deep neural image classifiers outputting incorrect labels due to small, human-imperceptible input perturbations, researchers propose fine-tuning pretrained models with curvature regularization, or CURE (https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.09716). The mechanism addresses the problem that standard-trained networks have high input-space curvature around data points (https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.09716). Because small loss curvature correlates with increased adversarial robustness, CURE enforces local linearity by penalizing Hessian eigenvalues, discouraging large eigenvalues in any direction (https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.09716). This curvature penalty is implemented via a finite-difference Hessian Frobenius norm penalty that estimates Hessian-vector products (https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.09716). By decreasing the magnitude of Hessian eigenvalues, the model expands the radius of adversarially safe regions and mimics the benefits of adversarial training more efficiently (https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.09716). Additionally, the text proposes conducting full adversarial training from random initialization, which involves generating multi-step PGD perturbations during each iteration and optimizing the model on these adversarial examples (https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.09716)."
